# Using crossref for building a bib

## tl;dr

This is based on a one-liner post. Provided a DOI of a paper or an article, the
following will use the crossref API [[1]](https://www.crossref.org) and display
the information regarding the document such as author, year, publication, etc.

```bash
#DOI=DOI of a paper
DOI='DOI';
curl -s "https://api.crossref.org/works/${DOI}/transform/application/x-bibtex"
```

The following is a bash function which makes use of this one-liner to do
slightly complicated tasks such as appending the crossref info of a document to
a file, selectively displaying the information when not given a file, etc.

## Bash Function

The `file` variable is initially empty, this is to store the file name the
user would provide upon calling the function `doi2bib` with the flag `-f`.
Likewise `info` is an empty string which would hold the information tag
such as author, year, publisher, volume etc., provided by the user with the
flag `-i`. The flags `-f` and `-i` are mutually exclusive in the following
script. Flag `-d` is mandatory since this specifies the `DOI` of the document
in question.

```bash
doi2bib() {
  local doi="" info="" file=""
  local api="https://api.crossref.org/works" trans="transform/application/x-bibtex"
  while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
    case "$1" in
      -h|--help)
        echo "usage: doi2bib -d DOI [-i FIELD | -f FILE]"
        return 0 ;;
      -d|--doi)  doi=${2-};  shift ;;
      -i|--info) info=${2-}; shift ;;
      -f|--file) file=${2-}; shift ;;
      *) echo "doi2bib: unknown option $1" >&2; return 1 ;;
    esac
    [ $# -gt 0 ] && shift
  done
  if [ -z "$doi" ]; then
    echo "doi2bib: no DOI given, use -d DOI" >&2; return 1
  fi

  local bib
  bib=$(curl -sfL "$api/$doi/$trans") || {
    echo "doi2bib: crossref has no BibTeX for $doi" >&2; return 1
  }

  if [ -n "$file" ]; then
    printf '\n%s\n' "$bib" >> "$file"
  elif [ -n "$info" ]; then
    grep -oE "$info=(\{[^}]*\}|[^,} ]*)" <<< "$bib"
  else
    printf '%s\n' "$bib"
  fi
}
```

A few typical examples of the usage of the above script are given as follows.

To show the full info on the terminal:

```bash
doi2bib -d https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jssc.2016.08.030
```

To show the selective info on the terminal:

```bash
doi2bib -d https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jssc.2016.08.030 -i author
```

To append the full information in the bibtex format to a file named
`mypaper.bib`:

```bash
doi2bib -d https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jssc.2016.08.030 -f mypaper.bib
```

